WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.290
Every time a JVM starts, it redoes the same work.

2
00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:13.103
The first time code touches a class, the JVM loads it, links it, and resolves its references.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.741
The first call of a lambda does more.

4
00:00:15.741 --> 00:00:21.548
Its invokedynamic instruction runs a bootstrap method that generates the lambda's class.

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:31.907
On JDK 25, one start of a 300-class app took 431 ms of wall time, and the next start pays it all again.

6
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.281
Project Leyden moves that work out of production runs.

7
00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:40.683
Its first result is the AOT cache, which you build in three phases.

8
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.634
First, a training run.

9
00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:46.339
You run the app with a representative workload.

10
00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:55.640
The JVM records what the app loaded and linked into an AOT configuration file, and on JDK 25 also its method profiles.

11
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:57.094
Second, assembly.

12
00:00:57.094 --> 00:00:59.908
The JVM turns that file into the cache.

13
00:00:59.908 --> 00:01:02.073
Your application does not run.

14
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.416
Third, production.

15
00:01:03.416 --> 00:01:05.980
Every start after that names the cache.

16
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:11.911
On JDK 25, one command with AOTCacheOutput does the first two phases.

17
00:01:11.911 --> 00:01:16.740
It trains, then starts a second JVM to assemble the cache.

18
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.127
The cache stores the results of start-up work from the training run.

19
00:01:22.127 --> 00:01:24.274
Classes already loaded and linked.

20
00:01:24.274 --> 00:01:27.810
Symbolic references already resolved to the real method.

21
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.020
Lambda call sites already resolved.

22
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.172
Objects the JVM would build at start-up, such as its module graph.

23
00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:40.069
And on JDK 25, the method profiles that tell the JIT what is hot.

24
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:47.379
In production, the JVM loads and links every cached class before main even starts.

25
00:01:47.379 --> 00:01:50.235
Then main runs without stopping for them.

26
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:57.190
For the 300-class app, wall time fell from 431 ms to 98.

27
00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:05.273
CPU time fell from 504 ms to 81, and peak memory from 64 to 42 MiB.

28
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:08.864
Code the training run never touched gets none of this.

29
00:02:08.864 --> 00:02:10.940
It is resolved lazily, as before.

30
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.212
So train with a workload that looks like production.
